ese will be  big data processing tools using our  t-bind for platform  but also scripting in python and r and  so what we hope  is that as a result of this particular  program  you will cover the following topics so  introduction to data science and  bioinformatics how the two are related  but also what are the specific  aspects of data science that typically  are not so  covered as well in bioinformatics itself  we'll talk about data processing  cleaning and exploration  the applications of data science and  biomedicine we'll use several examples  for that  we will talk about different types of  machine learning algorithms and we will  apply them to next generation sequencing  data  we'll talk about the challenge of  integrating various signals from  x omics or multi-omics data sets and  then we will also talk about the subject  of deep learning  and how that could be applied to omix  but also to clinical phenotypes  so as a result what we hope to do is  connect the research question or the  real need that exists out there  with different aspects of data analysis  that has  an impact on how we think about the data  or the experimentation that  generates that data how do we explore  that data and discover patterns that  turn into hypotheses  how can we test those hypotheses and  what that can reveal about  different types of biological  experiments producing high throughput  data  that oftentimes does not necessarily  fit our knowledge and so how this  data-driven  approach to data analysis is critical  for discovery and how data science plays  a major role  in that process so let me give you  an example of why data science is such  an important part and how does it apply  to such  intuitive applications like precision  medicine so  as we all know precision medicine is the  future of medicine in the sense that we  are relying more and more on complex  data generated  for clinicians to make decisions and  these decisions could be about treatment  it could be about outcomes it could be  risk stratification  and so in that process we are also  talking about data becoming more and  more precise  or also complex and so when we think of  traditional medicine  which is medicine based on symptoms  those are at the top of the iceberg or  the clinical data that we can see and  observe  the manifestation of disease and  symptoms that are  clearly identifiable and we want to  connect them with the underlying  molecular drivers of that process to  be able to intervene so to be able to  spot specific patterns  that can reveal some actionable insights  that is one example another example  where  machine learning and data science are  very applicable  is drug discovery and drug repurposing  where  the length of time from  ideas about what might work to actually  getting a drug on the market  is not only expensive but also very long  and that's where  data science and the application of  machine learning will play a major role  so we'll talk about some examples in  that  but also in clinical trials how going  from  recruiting patients for clinical trials  with  a broad kind of high level  symptom-based recruitment is also  changing because now they're being  recruited with  biomarker and that makes those very  precise and that makes that also very  different  from the traditional kind of landscape  of patients  and disease stratification so we will  also talk in this  program about different types of  vomiting data so as you probably know  omix data  could be classified into several  different types for example phenomics  talks about phenotypic data  genomics talks about dna variation trend  epigenomics talks about epigenetic  regulation and different signals that we  can capture about chromatin  structures and accessibility of dna we  can also talk about the result of that  regulation which results in  expression of mrna and other types of  rnas that could be collectively called  transcriptomics and so when we talk  about biomedical omics data  we talk about different examples with  diagnostics for cancer biology  basic biology where we try to understand  single cell and tissue composition  or even looking at the impact of  different variations at the microbiome  and for example immunity to infection  now what characterizes  all of these examples is the  availability of unstructured data so  many times we rely on data that has been  generated by processes which we can call  high throughput  and what that means is that they are  automated and because of that automation  a lot of the data is generated in  unstructured formats which  for example could mean the collection of  short reads  that contain sequences abundances and  the  process of taking that unstructured data  and turning it into something useful is  what we call  processing so for example we can process  whole exome sequencing and whole genome  sequencing to identify the actual  mapped reads to a reference genome and  then we can ask the question well  where are the changes that are  significant we can talk about dna  methylation  and chip sequencing where we take a look  at  immunoprecipitation and methylation  processes that  help us extract meaningful insights  about epigenomic regulation or  epigenetic regulation sorry  we can look at rna sequencing or levels  of gene expression  and we can also look at peak calling for  example for proteomics and metabolomics  trying to understand  what kinds of proteins and metabolites  are being  expressed in the sample so taking that  raw data  from images or reads and turning it  into structured data is basically  converting that raw data into  a table and once that table is there  we need to understand the different  aspects  of that data which might include numeric  data  continuous but also categorical where we  have  names of samples groups of samples names  of genes or proteins  or whatever else it might be and so in  the context of data science for  biomedical discovery  we will talk about how w